from h3 import L
g='8'
T="a block-based tool or a text-based language such as Python"
L(g,1,'scoping-a-program','Scoping a Program with Computational Thinking',['8.AP.1a','8.AP.1b','8.AP.1c'],
"The student will use pattern recognition, decomposition, and abstraction to scope a program for creative expression or scientific exploration.",
["Paper and pencil","Sticky notes","A project idea"],
["algorithms-and-programming","computational-thinking","decomposition","abstraction"],'unplugged',
["Ask the student to bring an idea for a substantial program: an interactive story or game, a data explorer, or a simulation of a system with several interacting parts."],
(3,4,8,4),
"Ask, \"What is the difference between an idea for a program and a program you could actually finish in three weeks?\"",
["Introduce scope: deciding what the program will and will not do.","Decompose the idea into sub-components and then into the functions each needs.","Find patterns: repeated steps that become loops or shared functions, and similar objects that share a structure.","Abstract: decide what data represents each thing in the program and what is left out. State the assumptions.","Separate the essential version, the smallest program that fulfills the purpose, from features to add later."],
["The student writes the purpose in one sentence.","Build a decomposition tree to two levels.","Mark repeated patterns and name the shared functions.","List the data needed for each main element and draw a line around the essential version."],
"The student produces a scoping document: purpose, decomposition tree, repeated patterns, data abstractions with stated assumptions, and the boundary of the essential version.",
["Decomposition is complete to two levels.","Patterns are tied to loops or functions.","The essential version is realistic."],
["Scope a small program with three sub-components.","Decompose together."],
["Identify dependencies and a build order.","Write two alternative abstractions and choose between them."],
["Review how a published game or simulation scopes its first version.","Have someone challenge the scope and defend or adjust it."],
["A finished small program teaches more than an unfinished ambitious one.","Keep this document. It drives Lessons 2 through 6."])
L(g,2,'illustrating-complex-algorithms','Illustrating Complex Algorithms',['8.AP.1d'],
"The student will use pseudocode, a decision tree, or a flowchart to illustrate a complex problem as an algorithm.",
["Paper, pencil, ruler","The scoping document from Lesson 1","A reference card of flowchart symbols"],
["algorithms-and-programming","pseudocode","flowcharts","decision-trees"],'unplugged',[],
(3,5,8,4),
"Show a paragraph describing a complicated rule, such as how a game decides the winner of a round. Ask, \"Could you program this from the paragraph alone? What is unclear?\"",
["Compare the three tools. Pseudocode suits step-by-step logic with loops and functions. A decision tree suits a choice with many branching conditions. A flowchart suits a process where the path depends on conditions and loops back.","Set conventions: consistent indentation and keywords in pseudocode; one question per node in a tree; standard symbols and labeled arrows in a flowchart.","Model one complex rule in two forms and compare which is clearer.","Explain levels of detail: a top-level diagram of the whole program and a detailed one for each difficult part."],
["The student chooses the most complex part of their project.","Illustrate it with the tool that fits best.","Trace it with two test cases to check it.","Illustrate the top-level flow of the whole program in a second form."],
"The student delivers two illustrations, one top-level and one detailed, and explains why each form was chosen.",
["The algorithm is complete, with every path leading somewhere.","Conventions are followed.","The choice of form is justified."],
["Illustrate a moderately complex rule in pseudocode.","Provide a partly completed flowchart."],
["Illustrate the same algorithm in all three forms and compare.","Have someone else implement a part from the illustration alone."],
["Illustrate a real-world process, such as how a refund request is handled.","Find a published flowchart and critique it."],
["If the illustration cannot be traced, the algorithm is not yet understood.","Different parts of one program may be best shown in different forms."],prereq=["Lesson 1: Scoping a Program"])
L(g,3,'data-types-in-and-out','Data Types In and Out, and the Plan',['8.AP.2a','8.AP.2b'],
"The student will describe the input and output of various data types in a program and plan an algorithm that uses them.",
["Paper and pencil","The illustrations from Lesson 2","A reference list of data types in your chosen language"],
["algorithms-and-programming","data-types","input","output","design-document"],'unplugged',
["Make a reference list for your tool: integer, decimal, string, Boolean, and list or array, with one example of each."],
(3,5,7,4),
"Ask, \"A program asks for your age and you type 'thirteen.' What should it do?\"",
["Review the data types: integers, decimal numbers, strings, and Booleans. Add lists, which hold many values in order.","Explain input: data arrives from a user, a file, or a sensor, often as text, and must be converted and checked.","Explain output: the program presents data as text, numbers formatted for reading, graphics, sound, or a file. The same value can be shown in different forms.","Explain why types matter: each supports different operations, and mixing them causes errors.","Model an input and output table for a program: each item, its type, where it comes from, and how it is checked or shown."],
["The student builds the input and output table for their project.","For each input, decide what counts as valid.","Add the variables and their types to the plan.","Update the pseudocode or flowchart to include input checking and output formatting."],
"The student completes the plan: an input and output table with data types, a variable list, and the algorithm including functions, loops, conditionals, and input checks.",
["Each input and output has a stated type.","Validation is planned.","The plan includes all required structures."],
["Plan a program with two inputs and one output.","Fill in the table together."],
["Plan a list that stores many values and a loop that processes it.","Plan for reading from or writing to a file."],
["Examine a form on a website and list the data type of each field.","Find a real error message caused by the wrong data type."],
["Input checking is where plans most often have gaps.","Lists appear here because the standard says various data types."],prereq=["Lesson 2: Illustrating Complex Algorithms"])
L(g,4,'write-and-test','Write It and Test It',['8.AP.2c','8.AP.3a'],
"The student will write and test an algorithm in a programming language, producing a program that contains multiple control structures, functions, and several data types.",
["A computer with "+T,"The plan from Lesson 3"],
["algorithms-and-programming","programs","functions","testing","iterative-design"],'device',
["Make sure the programming tool is ready and the student's plan is at hand."],
(2,3,10,5),
"Ask, \"Looking at your plan, what is the smallest piece you could build and test in ten minutes?\"",
["Set the working method: build one function at a time, test it, then connect it.","Review writing a test before the code: decide the inputs and expected results first.","Encourage comments that explain the purpose of each function.","Remind the student to save versions as they go."],
["The student writes the input handling with checks and tests it with valid and invalid data.","Write the first function and test it alone.","Write the main loop and conditional that use it.","Run the essential version end to end."],
"The student continues to a working essential version, then lists the tests run so far and their results.",
["The program follows the plan.","Multiple control structures, functions, and data types are used.","Each part is tested as it is built."],
["Build a reduced version with one function.","Pair on the first function."],
["Add a list and process it with a loop.","Add a second function that calls the first."],
["Continue building over several sessions.","Keep a development journal."],
["This lesson is mostly hands-on time. It may take more than one session.","Insist on a test for each function before moving on."],prereq=["Lesson 3: Data Types In and Out"])
L(g,5,'trace-and-diagnose','Trace and Diagnose',['8.AP.3b','8.AP.3c'],
"The student will trace programs to predict outcomes and analyze outcomes to identify logic and syntax errors.",
["A computer with the programming tool","The student's program","Two short programs with errors, prepared by you","Paper for trace tables"],
["algorithms-and-programming","tracing","debugging","errors"],'device',
["Prepare two short programs: one with a function and a loop that has an off-by-one error, and one with a syntax error and a wrong condition."],
(3,4,8,5),
"Ask, \"A loop is meant to run ten times and runs nine. How would you find out why without guessing?\"",
["Review tracing with functions: track each variable, including values passed to and returned from functions.","Name common logic errors: off-by-one in loops, reversed comparisons, wrong order of operations, a variable not reset, and the wrong variable used.","Review syntax errors and reading error messages: the message, the line, and the cause may be just before that line.","Introduce tools: printing values at key points, and a debugger that steps through code if your tool has one."],
["The student traces the first program and predicts its output.","Run it, compare, and locate the off-by-one error.","Fix the syntax error in the second using the message.","Add print statements to find the wrong condition."],
"The student traces one function of their own program for an edge case, predicts the outcome, runs it, and reports any error found with its type.",
["Traces track every relevant variable.","Errors are classified as logic or syntax.","Diagnosis uses evidence, not guesswork."],
["Trace a program with one loop and no functions.","Provide a partly filled trace table."],
["Use a debugger to step through code.","Find an error that appears only with certain inputs."],
["Debug an open-source beginner program.","Teach someone the difference between the two kinds of error."],
["Off-by-one errors are the classic loop mistake. Tracing first and last passes finds them.","Printing values is simple and remains useful at every level."],prereq=["Lesson 4: Write It and Test It"])
L(g,6,'validate-and-improve','Validate and Improve',['8.AP.3d','8.AP.3e'],
"The student will analyze and describe a program's results to assess their validity and revise the algorithm to resolve errors or produce the desired outcomes.",
["A computer with the programming tool","The student's program","Paper for a test plan","One or two testers"],
["algorithms-and-programming","validation","testing","revision","iterative-design"],'device',
["Arrange for someone to test the program.","Prepare a test plan template: case, input, expected result, actual result, pass or fail."],
(3,4,9,4),
"Ask, \"How many tests are enough to say a program works?\"",
["Define validity: results are correct and meet the purpose stated in the scoping document.","Teach test design: typical cases, edge cases at the boundaries, and invalid input.","Explain describing results: state what happened precisely, with values.","Explain revising the algorithm: when a test fails, decide whether the code or the plan is wrong. Update both. Rerun all tests after any change."],
["The student writes a test plan of at least six cases.","Run the plan and record results.","A tester uses the program while the student observes.","Choose the most important failure and revise the algorithm and code."],
"The student reruns the full test plan, makes a second revision if needed, and writes a summary: what was tested, what failed, what was changed, and why the outcomes are now valid.",
["The test plan covers typical, edge, and invalid cases.","Results are described with specifics.","Revisions are followed by full retesting."],
["Write four test cases.","Revise one failure together."],
["Automate a test by writing code that checks a function's results.","Measure the program against the original purpose and identify what is still missing."],
["Publish or share the finished program.","Write release notes describing the changes."],
["No number of tests proves a program correct. Good tests make errors unlikely.","This lesson completes the design cycle for the project."],prereq=["Lesson 5: Trace and Diagnose"])
L(g,7,'creative-commons','Creative Commons',['8.AP.4a','8.AP.4b'],
"The student will explain the role of Creative Commons licensing in the use and remixing of others' work and use Creative Commons assets in a programming project.",
["A connected device","A source of Creative Commons licensed images or sounds, chosen by you","The programming tool","A reference card of the license elements"],
["algorithms-and-programming","creative-commons","licenses","attribution","remix"],'device',
["Find a source of Creative Commons licensed media and check how it shows each item's license.","Make a reference card of the four elements: BY (give credit), SA (share adaptations under the same license), NC (non-commercial use only), ND (no derivatives, meaning no changes)."],
(3,5,8,4),
"Ask, \"You want others to use your photo, but you want credit and you do not want it sold. How could you tell the whole world that, without answering every request?\"",
["Explain the role: under ordinary copyright, all rights are reserved and each use needs permission. Creative Commons licenses let creators grant some permissions in advance, in a standard form anyone can read.","Teach the four elements and how they combine, such as BY, BY-SA, BY-NC, and BY-ND.","Define remixing: changing or combining existing work to make something new. ND licenses do not allow it. SA licenses require the remix to carry the same license.","Teach attribution for these works: title, author, source, and license."],
["The student finds two assets and reads each license.","Decide for each: may it be changed? used commercially? what must be done in return?","Add one asset to the programming project.","Write the attribution in the project's credits."],
"The student adds a second Creative Commons asset, attributes it, and explains what its license allows and what it would forbid.",
["The role of Creative Commons is explained.","License elements are interpreted correctly.","Assets are attributed with title, author, source, and license."],
["Use assets with the simplest license, BY.","Interpret one license together."],
["Remix an asset permitted by its license and license the result correctly.","Choose a Creative Commons license for your own project and explain why."],
["Find the license on a website, a photo, and a piece of music.","License one of your own creations."],
["Creative Commons builds on copyright. It does not replace it.","Checking for ND before changing an asset is the step most often missed."])
L(g,8,'remixing-code','Remixing Code with Credit',['8.AP.4c'],
"The student will use and remix code from another project within their own programming project and provide proper attribution.",
["A computer with the programming tool","A connected device","A source of shareable code chosen by you: the tool's community projects, a tutorial, or an open-source example"],
["algorithms-and-programming","remix","attribution","open-source"],'device',
["Find a small project or code example whose license or terms allow reuse and remixing.","Read it yourself so you can help the student understand it."],
(3,4,9,4),
"Ask, \"Is using someone else's code cheating? When is it, and when is it not?\"",
["Draw the line: reuse is normal practice when the code's license or terms allow it, the source is credited, and you understand what you are using. It is not acceptable when the terms forbid it, when it is passed off as your own, or when an assignment requires original work.","Teach the process: read the license or terms, read and understand the code, run it alone, adapt it, test it within your project, and attribute it.","Show attribution for code: a comment where the code appears naming the author, source, license, and what you changed.","Explain remixing: adapting the code to your needs, not copying it unchanged."],
["The student reads the borrowed code and explains what it does.","Run it on its own.","Remix it: change it to fit the project.","Integrate it, test it, and add the attribution comment."],
"The student demonstrates the integrated feature, shows the attribution, and explains which parts are borrowed, which are changed, and which are original.",
["The source's terms permit the reuse.","The student understands the borrowed code.","Attribution states source, author, license, and changes."],
["Remix a short code example of a few lines.","Read the code together."],
["Remix code from two sources and resolve the conflicts between them.","Contribute an improvement back to the original project, if it accepts contributions."],
["Study how a large open-source project credits its contributors.","Add a license and credits file to your project."],
["Understanding borrowed code before using it is part of using it responsibly.","Many assignments limit outside code. Checking the rules is part of the lesson."],prereq=["Lesson 7: Creative Commons"])
L(g,9,'improving-a-device','Improving a Device for Its Users',['8.CSY.1a','8.CSY.1b'],
"The student will analyze existing computing devices for advantages and limitations and recommend and design improvements based on how users interact with them.",
["Two computing devices to examine, such as a phone, tablet, laptop, smartwatch, or game controller","A person to observe using one","Paper for sketches","An observation sheet"],
["computing-systems","design","usability","user-needs"],'unplugged',
["Arrange for someone, ideally a person with different needs from the student's, such as a younger child or an older adult, to perform a few tasks on a device while the student observes.","Prepare an observation sheet: task, what the user did, where they struggled, what they said."],
(3,4,9,4),
"Ask, \"What is one thing about a device you use that you would change if you were its designer?\"",
["Teach analysis of a device: its purpose and users, its advantages, and its limitations, covering hardware such as size, weight, battery, buttons, and durability, and software such as menus, feedback, and accessibility.","Explain user interaction as evidence: watch real people use it and note where they hesitate, make errors, or work around problems.","Define a recommendation: a specific change, the user need it addresses, and the tradeoff it brings.","Show a design sketch with labels."],
["The student analyzes one device and lists its advantages and limitations.","Observe the user performing three tasks and complete the observation sheet.","Identify the two biggest difficulties.","Sketch one improvement and note its tradeoff."],
"The student presents a recommendation: the device, evidence from the observation, a labeled design for the improvement, and the tradeoffs.",
["The analysis covers hardware and software.","Recommendations are based on observed interaction.","The design addresses a specific user need."],
["Analyze one device and propose one change.","Use a prepared observation sheet with prompts."],
["Observe a second user with different needs and revise the design.","Build a paper or cardboard prototype and test it."],
["Read reviews of a device and sort the comments into advantages and limitations.","Look at how a device changed across versions."],
["Observation reveals problems that users would not think to mention.","Every improvement has a cost. Stating it is part of a recommendation."])
L(g,10,'writing-a-help-guide','Systematic Troubleshooting and Writing a Help Guide',['8.CSY.2a','8.CSY.2b'],
"The student will apply a systematic process to resolve hardware, software, and connectivity problems and design an end-user guide for resolving them.",
["A computing device","The family's troubleshooting log or knowledge base, if one exists","A word processor or paper","A person to test the guide"],
["computing-systems","troubleshooting","documentation","technical-writing"],'device-optional',
["Choose three common problems for the guide, one each for hardware, software, and connectivity.","Arrange for a non-expert to try the finished guide."],
(3,4,9,4),
"Ask, \"Have you ever followed instructions that skipped a step or used a word you did not know? What happened?\"",
["Review the systematic process: define the problem, list causes, order them, test one at a time, confirm, and document.","Define end user: the person who uses the device, who may have no technical background.","Teach guide design: a clear title for each problem, symptoms in plain words, numbered steps with one action each, what the user should see after each step, simplest and safest fixes first, pictures or diagrams, and when to stop and get help.","Explain testing a guide: watch someone follow it without helping them."],
["The student works through one problem systematically and records the steps.","Draft the guide section for that problem.","Add the other two problems.","A tester follows one section while the student observes."],
"The student revises the guide from the tester's experience and delivers the final version.",
["The troubleshooting process is systematic.","Steps are numbered, single-action, and in plain language.","The guide is revised after testing."],
["Write a guide for one problem.","Use a template with headings."],
["Add a decision tree at the front that directs users to the right section.","Add screenshots or diagrams."],
["Keep the guide with the family's devices.","Write a guide for a relative's most frequent problem."],
["Writing for a non-expert is harder than solving the problem. Testing with a real person shows the gaps.","A good guide says when to stop and ask for help."])
L(g,11,'cyber-attacks-and-their-impact','Cyber Attacks and Their Impact',['8.CYB.1a','8.CYB.1b'],
"The student will identify the impacts of hacking, ransomware, scams, phishing, and fake vulnerability scans, discuss the ethical and legal concerns, and describe how attacks affect a computing system.",
["Cards for five attack types","Paper for an impact chart","A news summary of a real incident, chosen by you"],
["cybersecurity","attacks","ethics","law"],'unplugged',
["Make cards: hacking, ransomware, scams, phishing, fake vulnerability scans.","Find a short, reliable news summary of one real incident."],
(3,6,7,4),
"Ask, \"A pop-up says your computer has 37 viruses and to click here to fix them. What is really going on?\"",
["Define each. Hacking: gaining unauthorized access to a system. Ransomware: malware that locks files and demands payment. Scams: deceptions to get money or information. Phishing: messages that impersonate trusted sources. Fake vulnerability scans: false warnings that push people to install harmful software or pay for unneeded fixes.","Describe impacts on people and organizations: lost money, stolen identity, lost data, services shut down, and damaged trust.","Describe effects on a computing system: files encrypted or deleted, data copied out, performance slowed, settings changed, control taken by an outsider, and spread to connected systems.","Raise legal concerns: unauthorized access and fraud are crimes with serious penalties. Raise ethical concerns: harm to people, paying ransoms, and the difference between attacking systems and testing them with permission."],
["The student completes the impact chart for each attack type: who is harmed, and how the system is affected.","Read the incident summary and identify the attack type and its impacts.","Discuss: is it ever ethical to access a system without permission to show it is insecure?","Discuss the legal consequences for the attacker."],
"The student describes two attack types with their impacts on people and on systems, and explains one ethical and one legal concern.",
["Attack types are defined accurately.","Impacts on both people and systems are described.","Ethical and legal concerns are distinguished."],
["Cover three attack types.","Use the incident summary as the main example."],
["Research the role of people who test security with permission.","Debate whether organizations should pay ransoms."],
["Follow reporting on one incident and track its consequences.","Explain fake vulnerability scans to a relative."],
["Describe what attacks do and why they matter. Do not teach how to carry them out.","Authorized security testing is a legal career. Unauthorized access is a crime. Make the distinction clear."])
L(g,12,'safe-practices-in-action','Safe Practices in Action',['8.CYB.1c','8.CYB.1e'],
"The student will compare safe and unsafe computing practices and model common practices that prevent cyber attacks.",
["A computing device","Practice cards in pairs","A checklist"],
["cybersecurity","prevention","safe-practices"],'device',
["Write paired cards showing an unsafe and a safe version of the same practice, such as reusing one password and using unique passwords; clicking a link in an unexpected message and going to the site directly; ignoring updates and installing them; using public Wi-Fi for banking and waiting for a trusted network; no backups and regular backups.","Prepare a checklist of prevention practices."],
(3,4,8,4),
"Ask, \"Which is more likely to protect you: expensive security software or your own habits? Why?\"",
["Compare each pair: what makes one safe and the other unsafe, and which attack the safe version prevents.","Group prevention practices: protect accounts (unique passwords, two-step sign-in), protect devices (updates, screen lock, security software), protect data (backups, encryption), and protect yourself (verify senders, think before clicking, limit sharing).","Define modeling a practice: doing it correctly yourself and showing others how."],
["The student sorts the cards and matches each safe practice to the attack it prevents.","On the device, the student models checking for updates and reviewing a sign-in setting, with you present.","The student models examining a suspicious message on paper: sender, link, urgency.","Complete the checklist for one family device."],
"The student teaches you three prevention practices as if you were new to them, demonstrating each, and explains what could happen without them.",
["Safe and unsafe practices are compared with reasons.","Practices are demonstrated correctly.","Each is linked to the attack it prevents."],
["Compare three pairs.","Model one practice."],
["Create a short training session for younger students or relatives.","Set up a backup routine with an adult."],
["Run the checklist on each family device.","Teach a grandparent to recognize a suspicious message."],
["The standard says \"model.\" The student should perform the practices, not only list them.","Changes to settings and accounts are made with you present."],prereq=["Lesson 11: Cyber Attacks and Their Impact"])
